<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George I of Greece</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1845">1845</a>–<a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George I, a Danish prince, was elected king of the Hellenes in 1863 after the deposition of Otto and reigned for fifty years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> ceded the Ionian Islands on his accession in 1864, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> was gained in 1881, and the Balkan Wars brought Macedonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. He accepted a democratic constitution in 1864 and was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a> in March 1913.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-i-of-greece.jpg" alt="George I of Greece" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_I_of_Greece,_c.1912.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born Prince William of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg-Glücksburg on 24 December 1845 at the Yellow Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, the second son of Prince Christian and Princess Louise of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>. The family was relatively obscure until 1852, when his father was designated heir to the childless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-vii-of-denmark">Frederick VII of Denmark</a>. William entered the Royal Danish Navy as a cadet.</p><p>After the expulsion of Otto in October 1862 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> held a plebiscite in which Prince Alfred, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, received over 95 per cent of the 240,000 votes; William received six. The ruling families of the great powers were barred from the Greek crown, and the choice fell on the Danish prince. The Greek National Assembly elected him unanimously on 30 March 1863.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>George reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> on 30 October 1863, learned Greek quickly and was often seen informally in the streets. He sent home Danish advisers who had become unpopular, and in October 1864 pressed the Assembly to finish its work; on 28 November he swore to a constitution that created a single chamber, the Vouli, elected by direct, secret, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-suffrage">universal male suffrage</a>. Governments were short-lived: 21 between 1864 and 1874. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charilaos-trikoupis">Charilaos Trikoupis</a> blamed the king in a newspaper article for imposing minority governments, George declared in 1875 that the leader of the parliamentary majority would in future be appointed prime minister.</p><p>Trikoupis and Theodoros Deligiannis alternated in office between 1882 and 1897. In 1896 the king presided over the opening of the revived Olympic Games in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. A revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> led in 1897 to a war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> that was lost by the end of April; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> paid an indemnity of 4 million Turkish pounds and Crete passed to international administration. George considered abdication, but regained esteem after he and his daughter Maria survived an attack by two riflemen on 27 February 1898.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In August 1909 a league of army officers demanded that the royal family lose their military commissions and attempted a coup. George gave way to calls for a revising assembly, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos">Eleftherios Venizelos</a> became prime minister in 1910. King and minister agreed on rebuilding the army, which entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a> on 9 November 1912 under Crown Prince Constantine. On 18 March 1913, walking near the White Tower without meaningful protection, George was shot in the back by Alexandros Schinas and died at once. Schinas fell to his death from a police station window six weeks later. The dynasty he founded reigned, with breaks, until the monarchy was abolished in 1973.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George I of Greece” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The National Assembly then chose <b>Prince William of Denmark</b>, who reigned as George I from 1863 to 1913 under the constitution of 1864, which established universal male suffrage and a single-chamber parliament.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>George I of Greece</b> opened the first modern Olympics at the Panathenaic Stadium.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-6">6 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Greeks deposed King Otto, Britain agreed to cede the islands to encourage the new <b>King George I</b>. Under the Treaty of London the islands joined the Kingdom of Greece in 1864, and Corfu's fortifications were partly demolished.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-states-of-the-ionian-islands">United States of the Ionian Islands</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.68,12.57&amp;year=1889" aria-label="Copenhagen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4824 265 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of George I of Greece"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5274 535h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5274 535h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.68,12.57&amp;year=1889">What was here in 1889? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
